	abstract = {In this paper, we propose a complete and robust ASIC architecture of adaptive-learning-rate GBDT design with early stopping technique, including both trainer and classifier circuits, abbreviated ALR-GBDT-EST. It primarily aims to efficiently detect cybersecurity attacks in the emerging AI-driven world. In addition, we develop two effective design techniques to further elaborate our chip work, including Adaptive Learning Rate (ALR) and Early Stopping Technique (EST), thereby delivering a low-training-latency characteristic. With TSMC 40 nm multi-Vt CMOS technology, our ASIC work is well-implemented with a total core area of 0.581 mm<sup>2</sup> only, operating at a maximum frequency of 510 MHz on chip. In the system validation, our chip is fully verified with five representative cybersecurity datasets from Kaggle and UCI databases. As compared to the conventional GBDT, it is obvious that our high-flexibility chip work has less tree iterations and less tree layers. Accordingly, the speed-up ratio in training ranges from 1.1 to 6.5 times, successfully featuring a superior property of low training latency. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
