	abstract = {The growing reliance of power grid operations on digital communications, networked controllers, and inverterbased distributed energy resources (DERs) expands the cyberattack surface and motivates realistic, closed-loop cybersecurity assessment platforms. This paper introduces an edge AI-enabled cybersecurity experimentation framework implemented as a cyber-physical power system hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) testbed, integrating OPAL-RT real-time simulator, EXata CPS cyber emulator, real-time automation controller (RTAC), protection relay, and edge intelligent electronic device (IED). The simulator executes physical power grid with the network emulator that allows the simulation of real-time cyber intrusion. Time-synchronized measurements and supervisory controls are exchanged using C37.118 and DNP3, while a custom DNP3 module streams nodal measurement features to the edge IED for online inference. The controlled environment facilitates vulnerability assessments and real-time cyberattack impact analysis of DER-rich distribution grid. The testbed is validated on a modified IEEE 13-node and IEEE 123-node feeder implemented in RT-LAB, enabling HIL evaluation of both operational impacts and AI inference performance. Single and multi-wave coordinated attacks targeting DER inverter and network topology demonstrate the effectiveness of the testbed for real-time operational impact analysis. In parallel, single and multi-feature false data injection attacks are used to validate pretrained Random Forest model deployed on the edge IED, demonstrating high detection accuracy and low inference latency. Overall, the results confirm that the proposed framework enables synchronized real-time attack execution, offline data collection, measurable cyber-physical impact studies, and utilityoriented evaluation of edge-AI-based intrusion detection. © 1972-2012 IEEE.},
