	abstract = {With the increasing demand for cybersecurity, the in-depth exploration of entity recognition technology in open-source intelligence has received significant attention. In view of the characteristic of open-source intelligence, general entity recognition models often exhibit limitations. With the purpose of improving the performance of the model, this paper constructs a multi-granularity feature fusion model based on gating mechanism. Firstly, a pre-trained language model is utilized to transform the text into context-aware word embeddings. Perturbations are introduced in the word embedding layer to avoid dominance by non-entities during training and improve robustness. The part-of-speech, character, and syntax dependency features are then encoded separately and concatenated into a representation. A feature fusion method is designed to dynamically weight the output vectors of the pre-trained model and the previous representation. Subsequently, bidirectional sequence modeling is performed to focus on contextual information. Ultimately, the fused features are mapped to the label space and the vectors are decoded into the labels based on the global dependency. The performance of the model is evaluated on two public datasets. The experimental results demonstrate that the proposed model outperforms other competitive models in recent years. Furthermore, considering the scarcity of domain-specific datasets, this paper also proposes a hybrid data augmentation method and validates its effectiveness. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
