	abstract = {This narrative review synthesizes recent advances in applying Artificial Intelligence (AI), the Internet of Things (IoT), and smart sensing materials to drinking-water treatment. Across documented deployments, AI–IoT architectures increasingly support predictive control at raw-water intake, where short-horizon forecasting enables proactive blending and dosing adjustments. Pilot studies report coagulant reductions of ∼10–22% while maintaining settled turbidity below ∼1 NTU, demonstrating measurable chemical savings under defined raw-water conditions. Low-power IoT sensing networks, typically operating near ∼29 W and achieving telemetry ranges of up to ∼2 km (LoRaWAN), further support continuous monitoring with practical energy demand. Complementing these platforms, smart sensing materials, including multispectral optical systems and electrochemical probes, provide enhanced microbial and chemical detection. Reported limits of detection (LOD) span sub-micromolar to micromolar ranges, sufficient for early anomaly screening and suitability for emerging contaminants. When coupled with machine learning, ensemble models and digital-twin workflows show high predictive performance for fouling evolution, flux decline, and Water Quality Index (WQI) estimation. Yet metrics such as root mean square error (RMSE), Nash–Sutcliffe efficiency (NSE), and feature-attribution reliability require validation across diverse field conditions and sensor-drift scenarios. Key challenges persist, including calibration drift, heterogeneous data standards, cybersecurity vulnerabilities, and limited cross-site benchmarking. Taken together, current evidence suggests meaningful potential for cleaner production through intelligent sensing and predictive analytics; however, broader multi-site trials, standardized reporting, and clearer regulatory frameworks will be essential for transitioning prototype systems into reliable, full-scale operational tools. Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier Ltd.},
