	abstract = {The rapid evolution of advanced persistent threats (APTs) poses a serious challenge to cybersecurity. Unlike most current studies that focus on detecting a single attack event, understanding the tactics and techniques used by attackers to implement APT activities within an organization, such as the MITRE ATT&CK framework, is important to improve security operations. In existing APT tactic/technique mapping methods, rule-based approaches heavily rely on expert knowledge, resulting in coarse tactical classification granularity and strong subjectivity in rules, making it difficult to effectively identify fine-grained techniques; traditional machine learning methods, on the other hand, suffer from limited context representation capabilities, leading to low recognition rates and insufficient interpretability. In response to the above problems, we propose APTREC, the first attempt to leverage large language models (LLMs) for recognizing APT tactics/techniques from subgraphs. To address the data sparsity issue, APTREC constructs a tactic-aligned subgraph dataset that aligns with the MITRE ATT&CK framework. To improve precision, APTREC employs a two-stage inference architecture. A technique recognition module parses attack context semantic features, while a tactic reasoning module filters the optimal tactics. Experimental results demonstrate that APTREC achieves a 13% F1-score improvement and a 9% recall rate increase in APT tactic recognition compared to existing representative methods, and it also offers strong explainability. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
	author_keywords = {Advanced persistent threat (APT); ATT&CK framework; Attack tactic recognition; Interprebility; Large language model (LLM); Tactical subgraphs},
