	abstract = {The rapid integration of artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) in the petroleum industry is creating a complex, evolving research landscape. While there are numerous applied studies, a systematic synthesis of the field's intellectual structure, key drivers, and research gaps is lacking. This study conducts a comprehensive scientometric and integrated knowledge management analysis of 1379 publications from the Scopus database (2012–2024) to map the evolution of machine learning applications aimed at improving the productivity of the petroleum industry. Using VOSviewer and RStudio, we map publication trends, author, country, and institutional collaboration networks, map international collaboration patterns, and analyze topic clusters using keyword co-occurrence. Our findings quantitatively identify China and its leading petroleum universities as the dominant force in research output and impact. This analysis demonstrates a clear thematic progression from early predictive models to deep learning, real-time optimization, and intelligent maintenance. Importantly, we move beyond descriptive bibliometrics by building these insights into a structured knowledge management framework. We identify critical research gaps—such as small-sample learning for complex reservoirs, real-world field validation, and cybersecurity—and propose targeted knowledge management strategies (such as expert communities of practice, trend-integrated dashboards) to fill these gaps and accelerate the transition from academic research to operational innovation. This work provides researchers with a clear roadmap of the field, provides industry professionals with an evidence-based guide to technology adoption, and equips policymakers with insights into the global dynamics of digital transformation in the energy sector. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
