	abstract = {Ransomware is one of the biggest threats in the current cybersecurity landscape with severe attacks causing millions of United States Dollars in damages. Many have looked to machine learning and artificial intelligence to identify and prevent these costly attacks. This review gathers and analyzes one hundred and five research papers to understand what machine learning methods are reporting the best performance for detecting ransomware, and what advancements are being made in the field. The papers were then separated into groups depending on the contents of the research. The suggested frameworks and reviews are judged qualitatively, focusing on how they detect ransomware, how the models are added to the network, and the benefits and drawbacks of each implementation. Experiment groups were judged quantitatively by analyzing the performance of the suggested classifiers in their various tests to detect ransomware. This data was then analyzed further by generating median, mode, and standard deviation to understand the variance in ransomware detection in major research. This paper found that lightweight models using simple classifiers such as Random Forest reported similar scores to more complex models such as Neural Networks and should be focused upon in further research. This highlights two areas of future work, creating lightweight scalable detection systems or creating more centralized and learning focused systems. Furthermore, this review found that many experiments do not fully list common AI metrics necessary to understand the performance of a model, and future research should ensure these metrics are presented alongside more tests for external validity. © 2026, University of Bahrain. All rights reserved.},
