	affiliations = {School of Information Engineering, Huzhou University, Huzhou, China; School of Computing, University of Eastern Finland, Kuopio, Finland},
	abstract = {With the rapid development of the Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT), the propagation of malware has emerged as a significant challenge in cybersecurity. Although existing infectious disease propagation models have played a crucial role in analyzing the spread of malware, they are limited in their ability to address the dynamic and adversarial nature of the attacker–defender interaction. These models fail to capture the complex strategic behavior between attackers and defenders. To address this gap, we propose an enhanced SIQR (Susceptible–Infected–Quarantined–Recovered) model, introducing a Quarantined state (Q) to more accurately represent the impact of quarantining a compromised device. Additionally, we introduce the concept of device degree to quantify the connectivity and influence of a device within the network, thereby enabling a more precise simulation of the malware propagation process. Furthermore, this paper integrates a stochastic games-assisted parameter into the infection rate to model the strategic interactions between attackers and defenders. By analyzing the model’s stability and equilibrium, calculating the critical conditions for the propagation of malware, and conducting extensive experimental validations, we demonstrate that the SIQR model effectively captures the dynamics of IIoT malware propagation. This model provides a robust theoretical foundation for mitigating the spread of malware in the IIoT. © 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
