	abstract = {The rapid evolution of malware and its use of obfuscation, polymorphism, and code reuse have rendered traditional detection mechanisms inadequate. Recent deep-learning and graph-based methods demonstrate strong potential but often rely on single-source features, topology-only reduction, and post-hoc explainability, limiting robustness and interpretability. This paper introduces a novel Hybrid Static–Dynamic Semantic Graph Neural Network (HSD-GNN) designed to unify structural and behavioral representations for explainable malware detection. The framework integrates Control Flow Graphs (CFGs), Function Call Graphs (FCGs), and Behavior Graphs (BGs) into a unified hybrid structure, employs a Semantic-Aware Graph Reduction (SAGR) mechanism for information-preserving pruning, and incorporates an Explainability-Guided Learning (EGL) feedback loop that links interpretability with model optimization. Experimental evaluations on BODMAS, Dike Dataset, and PMML demonstrate superior accuracy (98.4%), reduced model size (−25%), and high explainability fidelity (91.7%) compared with recent GNN-based detectors. Statistical and cross-dataset validation confirm the stability and generalization of the proposed approach. Overall, HSD-GNN establishes a scalable and interpretable paradigm for next-generation malware detection, advancing the integration of graph learning and explainable artificial intelligence in cybersecurity. © This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License. License details: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/},
