	abstract = {AbstractReinforcement Learning (RL) is emerging as a key approach for autonomous Penetration Testing, enabling agents to learn offensive actions through interaction with simulated networks. However, current RL environments differ widely in realism, attack coverage, and scalability, creating a significant gap between experimental performance and real-world applicability. This work evaluates the internal design of state-of-the-art RL Pentesting simulators, identifying limitations in their action models, MITRE ATT&CK coverage, emulation support, reward mechanisms, network scalability, and environment fidelity. Our contribution is a technical comparison that isolates which environments best support each feature for practical Autonomous Pentesting. Results show that no simulator leads in all area. NASimEmu, Caldera, and Cyberwheel provide the highest environment fidelity; Caldera, Cyberwheel, and CybORG offer the broadest MITRE ATT&CK coverage; and NASim, MulVAL, and CyberBattleSim achieve the best scalability through lightweight graph-based designs. © 2026 The Author(s). Published by Elsevier B.V. This is an open access article under the CC BY license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/},
