	abstract = {This research conducts a thorough bibliometric analysis on leadership in the era of cyber threats, as strategic oversight is identified as a core element in managing organizational resilience and digital safety. The body of knowledge on cybersecurity leadership has expanded, but it is conceptually fragmented and lacks integration between institutional, technological, and stakeholder dimensions. Based on a systematic review of 474 peer-reviewed articles using VOSviewer and Bibliometrix, the work pinpoints three thematic clusters: (1) leadership drivers, barriers, and institutional context in cybersecurity; (2) innovation, resilience, and organizational performance; and (3) stakeholder engagement, risk governance, and social aspects of cyber threat mitigation. These clusters hint at a shift of academic attention from compliance-oriented security toward active, adaptive leadership in uncertain environments. Underlying a large body of extant scholarship is a theoretical focus on institutional theory, stakeholder theory, and the resource-based view. But there is a clear move toward digital transformation, catalyst AI, and collaborative governance. The review provides a systematic knowledge map of the domain of cybersecurity leadership, where we identify future research areas around ethical AI, inclusive supervisory frameworks, and cross-sectoral coordination. It highlighted the urgent necessity of leadership approaches that are nimble, digitally literate, and sensitive to an evolving cyber threats cape and therefore deliver value for both scholarly reflection and applied practice in complex cyber risk environments. © Copyright 2025 Taylor & Francis–All rights reserved.},
