	affiliations = {School of Computer Science and Engineering, Northeastern University, Shenyang, China; Hebei Key Laboratory of Marine Perception Network and Data Processing, Northeastern University at Qinhuangdao, Qinhuangdao, China; College of Cyber Security, Jinan University, Guangzhou, China},
	abstract = {AI-powered phishing URLs (A-PURLs) have shifted URL attacks from simple lexical obfuscation to systematic structural mutation, where adversaries continuously synthesize new variants that invalidate static detection patterns. This evolution exposes a major weakness of existing URL-based detectors: they rely heavily on surface semantics and often fail to generalize under spatial adversarial shifts and temporal concept drift. To address these challenges, we present Anti-APhish, an integrated detection and continual adaptation framework. We first introduce Phishing Mutation Grammar (PMG), a structured mutation framework that characterizes multi-level structural transformations observed in A-PURLs. PMG enables controlled adversarial augmentation training, thereby reducing generator-specific shortcut learning. Built upon this, we design MASCoNet, a convolution–attention collaborative architecture that learns mutation-topology-aware representations from URL sequences. To address evolving attacks over time, we further develop a lightweight incremental adaptation scheme that detects concept drift and updates the model through clustered core samples and low-rank adaptation, reducing manual labeling effort by 88% while preserving performance. Extensive experiments are conducted using heterogeneous datasets with strict distributional, temporal, and attack-pattern separation. Experimental results show that Anti-APhish consistently outperforms SOTA baselines under all evaluation scenarios. Anti-APhish achieves 97.57% accuracy and 0.9517 MCC under 20% A-PURL exposure and maintains 94.76% accuracy and 0.8976 MCC in zero-shot evaluation against LLM-PURLs. These results suggest that modeling structural mutation topology, rather than relying solely on surface semantics, is an important factor for robust and generalizable A-PURL detection. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
