	abstract = {The increasing integration of Electric Vehicle (EV) charging stations into modern smart grids has raised significant cybersecurity concerns, particularly the vulnerability to Distributed Denial-of-Service (DoS) attacks that can disrupt charging operations and system stability. Existing intrusion detection methods often rely on computationally intensive models or limited feature extraction, which restrict their applicability in real-time, resource-constrained EV charging environments. This study addresses the research question of how lightweight yet accurate intrusion detection can be achieved for EV charging infrastructure using system-level behavioral data. This study proposes a hybrid intrusion detection framework that integrates Transformer-based feature extraction with a streamlined Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) classifier. The methodology leverages kernel-level event logs to capture both temporal dependencies and complex feature interactions. A structured preprocessing pipeline is applied to ensure feature leakage prevention, data normalization, and stratified cross-validation. The model is trained and evaluated using data collected from real Electric Vehicle Supply Equipment (EVSE) hardware in a controlled experimental environment. The novelty of the proposed approach lies in combining Transformer-based representation learning with a lightweight MLP decision module for efficient, on-device deployment in EV charging stations. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves near-perfect performance, with approximately 100% accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score, along with strong PR-AUC and ROC-AUC values. The system is also optimized for decentralized deployment, enabling real-time intrusion detection without reliance on centralized servers. Overall, the proposed framework provides an efficient and scalable solution for enhancing the resilience of EV charging infrastructure against DoS attacks. © 2026 The Author(s). Publishe