	abstract = {This study explores the impact of artificial intelligence (AI) on the military sector, focusing on its influence on warfare, strategic management, and national security policy, with Kazakhstan as a case study. The research assessed AI’s role in military evolution, strategic positioning, and related risks and opportunities. The methodology included content and institutional analysis, discursive reconstruction, and conceptual modelling based on strategic documents and international agreements. Findings reveal that AI has created a new warfare paradigm where information dominance, algorithmic superiority, and autonomous systems are crucial. The United States focuses on innovation within democratic norms, China emphasizes military-civil integration, and the EU leads in ethical frameworks. Kazakhstan, however, remains technologically reliant on China and Russia, lacking a national strategy for military AI, which weakens its sovereignty. The study concludes that sustainable security requires integrating ethics, technology, and global regulation, offering a foundation for Kazakhstan’s policy on military AI. © Aigerim Mustafina and Aibolat Dalelkhan, 2026. Published with license by Koninklijke Brill BV. This work is published by Koninklijke Brill BV. Koninklijke Brill BV incorporates the imprints Brill, Brill Nijhoff, Brill Schöningh, Brill Fink, Brill mentis, Brill Wageningen Academic, Vandenhoeck & Ruprecht, Böhlau and V&R unipress. Koninklijke Brill BV reserves the right to protect the publication against unauthorized use and to authorize dissemination by means of offprints, legitimate photocopies, microform editions, reprints, translations, and secondary information sources, such as abstracting and indexing services including databases. Requests for commercial re-use, use of parts of the publication, and/or translations must be addressed to Koninklijke Brill BV.},
