	abstract = {The complexity of cyber environment has exacerbated the frequent cybersecurity problems. As the public attach importance to information security, processing cyber threat intelligence is very critical for analyzing cyber-attack behaviors. For unstructured cyber threat intelligence, named entity recognition can effectively analyze the text structure and content. Therefore, this paper proposes an end-to-end entity recognition model for cyber threat intelligence. First, the input text is encoded into vectors, and perturbations are introduced to the word embeddings, which will enhance the robustness of the model by incorporating adversarial samples during training. Next, local features are extracted without increasing the number of convolutional kernels, followed by fusion with global contextual features. Subsequently, we increase the number of Attention heads to assign different weights to word vectors to better model long-range dependency. Finally, a linear chain conditional random field is used to optimize the sequence prediction. The experiments are conducted on two public datasets. The results show that the proposed model achieved F<sub>1</sub> scores of 96.32% and 94.83% on two datasets, respectively, and the F<sub>1</sub> score improves by 1.52% and 0.85% compared to the best baseline, respectively. The paper also explores the possible factors that affect the performance of the proposed model, which provides a reference for later research on entity recognition in cyber threat intelligence. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
