	abstract = {This article examines the implications of artificial intelligence (AI) on employment law, arbitration, and cybersecurity within the United Arab Emirates (UAE). AI-driven human resource (HR) functions payroll, recruitment, performance evaluation are efficient, but can also contribute to cyberfinancial risks (such as payroll diversion, deep fakes), privacy concerns, and algorithmic discrimination/bias. The engineering and construction (E&C) industry in the UAE is particularly prone to such risks, as the vast project workforce, long chains of subcontracting, and digitized project-management systems form an overcrowded ecosystem of AI-enabled HR and payment systems. The existing system in UAE, fragmented across labor law, cybercrime law, and arbitration law, are not well placed to address these cross-cutting issues. The use of AI-generated and manipulated digital evidence in construction disputes is becoming more and more visible, but the admissibility and authentication of such evidence are not well-developed yet. This has placed employers, employees, and even tribunals in crossroads. The article recommends a unified regulatory framework able to establish AI-specific arbitration principles regarding the admissibility and authentication of AI-generated evidence; mandate risk assessment and incident reporting in HR systems; reinforce cybersecurity obligations in relation to AI-related threats; and embed AI ethics (fairness, transparency, accountability) into labor-law reforms. © 2026 American Society of Civil Engineers.},
