	abstract = {This study investigated the factors influencing artificial intelligence (AI) adoption for cybersecurity assurance in internal audit, focusing on capability dimensions, governance mechanisms, and institutional pressures within Ghana and comparable African contexts. An explanatory sequential mixed-methods design was employed, beginning with a structured survey analyzed using SmartPLS-based structural equation modeling (SEM) to test hypotheses, followed by thematic analysis of semi-structured interviews to explore barriers and enablers to capability-building. The quantitative findings demonstrated that technological robustness, human capital capacity, and organizational readiness significantly predicted AI adoption, while institutional pressures positively moderated these relationships. Governance mechanisms and trust in AI-generated evidence mediated the effectiveness of cybersecurity assurance. The qualitative phase provided contextual depth, highlighting barriers such as resource constraints, trust gaps, and policy inconsistencies, alongside enablers including leadership support and strategic capacity-building initiatives. Together, these findings offered a context-sensitive framework integrating capability, governance, and institutional dynamics, advancing theoretical understanding of AI adoption in audit functions and providing empirical evidence from underrepresented African economies. The study’s implications inform ICT policy development, organizational capacity-building, and audit innovation strategies, with the proposed model serving as a diagnostic tool for assessing readiness and guiding responsible AI integration in public sector assurance. © Copyright 2026 Taylor & Francis–All rights reserved.},
