	abstract = {Artificial Intelligence (AI) Technologies have established to be crucial tools in the process of digital transformation in governmental and private institutions in Jordan, especially in the fields related to Cyber Security, Financial Technologies, E-Government Systems, and Information Systems Governance. The mounting number of AI applications, however, has conveyed about legal, technical, cybersecurity issues around information asset protection, cyber-resilience, digital accountability and operational continuity. The goal of this study is to analyze the Jordanian legislator’s attitude of utilizing the AI techniques from an IT security perspective through looking into cybersecurity governance and legislation, IT auditing, and information systems protection in Jordanian institutions. The study is descriptive–analytical, as it relies on the analysis of current legal and cyber security texts about the governance of AI and cyber risk management. The results indicate that remaining laws and regulations in Jordan do not adequately address the emerging AI-driven cybersecurity threats, including data breaches, algorithm manipulation, digital fraud, and operational disruption. The study also has implications on the successful implementation of effective IT governance, auditing of cyber security, and internal control frameworks that can critically enhance institutional resilience and ensure a secure digital transformation. © Copyright 2026 Taylor & Francis–All rights reserved.},
