	affiliations = {Shanghai Jiao Tong University, School of Computer Science, Shanghai, China; Shanghai Key Laboratory of Integrated Administration Technologies for Information Security, Shanghai, 200240, China; University of Amsterdam, Amsterdam, 2586203, Netherlands},
	abstract = {Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) is rapidly transforming education management through advanced content generation, reasoning, and autonomous decision-support capabilities. While these technologies promise significant gains in efficiency, personalization, and scalability, their deployment also introduces a class of cognitive security risks that extend beyond traditional cybersecurity and data privacy concerns. These risks directly affect human cognition, judgment, learning integrity, and institutional governance, yet remain insufficiently systematized in existing research. This paper presents a comprehensive analysis of cognitive security in GenAI-enabled education management from a socio-technical and engineering management perspective. We propose a three-tiered taxonomy that categorizes risks into 1) internal security (model vulnerabilities such as jailbreaks, backdoors, and data poisoning), 2) interactional security (hallucination, bias, data leakage, and intellectual property infringement arising from human–AI interaction), and 3) external security (the weaponization of GenAI for fraud, disinformation, deepfakes, and malicious automation). Building on this taxonomy, we systematically review representative attack vectors and corresponding technical countermeasures, including robustness enhancement, inference-time monitoring, privacy-preserving generation, and content provenance mechanisms. Beyond technical mitigation, the paper advances a structured security governance framework tailored for educational institutions, integrating regulatory compliance, organizational policy design, and risk-based decision-making. By synthesizing global regulatory trends with practical management considerations, this work provides actionable guidance for education managers, technology developers, and policymakers seeking to deploy GenAI responsibly. Overall, the study contributes a unified conceptual foundation and governance roadmap to support secure, trustworthy, and sustainable adoption of GenAI in smart education systems. © 1973-2011 IEEE.},
