	abstract = {Volatile memory provides the most direct and clear view into a system’s runtime behavior. Yet, traditional forensics methods are prone to errors and remain fragile against modern obfuscation and injection techniques. This paper introduces a textual-attention transformer framework that treats raw memory bytes as linguistic tokens, allowing the model to read memory as text and infer contextual relationships across disjoint regions. The proposed model aligns positional encodings with memory addresses and learns to associate scattered structures—such as injected stubs, PE headers, and decryption routines—within a unified semantic space. Experiments on two publicly verifiable datasets, CIC-MalMem-2022 (multi-class) and NIST CFReDS Basic Memory Images (binary), demonstrate that this approach reconstructs malware behavior with ≈97% accuracy, outperforming CNN and LSTM baselines. Attention heatmaps reveal interpretable forensic cues that identify malicious regions, bridging AI and digital forensics. The proposed concept of textual self-attention for memory opens a new paradigm in automated memory analysis—transforming volatile memory into a readable, interpretable sequence for malware behavior reconstruction. © 2025 by the author.},
