	affiliations = {Department of Computer Science, German University of Technology in Oman, P.O. Box: 1816, Muscat, P.C 130, Oman; Faculty of Computer Studies, Arab Open University, P.O. Box 1596, Muscat, 122, Oman; Lappeenranta-Lahti University of Technology, Lappeenranta, 53850, Finland; Department of Computer Science, Abdul Wali Khan University, Mardan, 23200, Pakistan; Department of Computer Science, Bacha Khan University, Charsadda, 24420, Pakistan},
	abstract = {The growing prevalence of Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT) networks has intensified the demand for robust and scalable intrusion detection mechanisms. However, data privacy and protection with high detection accuracy remains a challenge. Centralized frameworks typically decentralize privacy and are not scalable. This study aims to propose a hybrid Convolutional Neural Networks - Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (CNN-Bi-LSTM)-based intrusion detection framework integrated with Federated Learning (FL) to address these challenges in IIoT environments. The framework combines the spatial feature extraction capabilities of Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) with the temporal dependency analysis of Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (Bi-LSTM) networks. To validate the framework, experiments were conducted using three benchmark IIoT datasets: X-IIoTID, WUSTL-IIoT, and Edge-IIoTset. Data preprocessing included normalization, feature encoding, and class balancing. FL was employed to preserve data privacy, with local models trained on edge devices and aggregated at a central server. The model's performance was evaluated against centralized learning approaches, considering accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and Area Under the Curve (AUC) metrics. The proposed framework demonstrated superior intrusion detection capabilities with an accuracy of 97.8 % on X-IIoTID and consistent AUC scores above 0.95 across all datasets. FL significantly reduced communication overhead while ensuring data privacy. The hybrid architecture detected diverse attack types, including Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS), Injection, Backdoor, and Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks. The hybrid CNN-Bi-LSTM model and the FL offer a way of securing the IIoT on a scalable and private level. The results show the possibility of combining progressive AI approaches with decentralized structures to strengthen IIoT security and effectiveness. © 2025 The Author(s)},
