	abstract = {With the increasing prevalence of phishing attacks, phishing webpage detection has become a critical challenge in the field of cybersecurity. Most existing phishing detection methods rely on URL or HTML features for training classification models. However, these methods have significant limitations when confronted with rapidly evolving phishing webpages: extracting only URL or HTML features fails to capture the multi-dimensional characteristics of phishing webpages, limiting the accuracy and comprehensiveness of detection; traditional multi-feature extraction methods have not effectively integrated features from different dimensions, resulting in information loss and semantic gaps. To address these issues, we propose a novel phishing webpage detection method-D-PhishNet. This method employs a dual-branch network architecture that combines Graph Neural Networks (GNN) and Transformer models. These models process the structured information of URLs and the textual content of HTML pages separately. Specifically, D-PhishNet effectively leverages the strengths of different network models for information extraction and enables the efficient extraction of multi-dimensional features from webpages. To further improve model performance, D-PhishNet introduces an improved feature fusion module that integrates features from different dimensions, effectively addresses the semantic gap issue in traditional methods. Furthermore, we propose a progressive training strategy that effectively addresses gradient conflicts and convergence issues in the end-to-end training of multi-branch models, significantly improving training efficiency and detection accuracy. Extensive experimental results on three benchmark datasets demonstrate that our proposed D-PhishNet consistently outperforms previous state-of-the art methods. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.},
