	abstract = {The rise of autonomous vehicles (AVs) has transformed the automotive industry, enhancing safety, efficiency, and driving convenience through technologies like adaptive cruise control, automatic emergency braking, and artificial intelligence (AI)-powered navigation. However, this reliance on interconnected systems and AI introduces significant cybersecurity risks. AVs depend on complex software, real-time data processing, and vehicle-to-everything (V2X) communication, making them vulnerable to cyberattacks that can compromise critical functions such as braking or steering. This study examines key cybersecurity threats in AVs, focusing on vulnerabilities in sensor networks, perception systems, and onboard control units. Attacks such as spoofing, jamming, and data injection can disrupt sensors like Light Detection and Ranging (LiDAR), radar, and cameras. Moreover, over-the-air (OTA) updates and cloud-based AI models present risks like remote code execution and adversarial attacks. To counter these threats, the study explores solutions including machine learning-based intrusion detection systems (IDS), anomaly detection, cryptographic protocols, and behavioral analysis. Hardware-based protections like Trusted Platform Modules (TPM) and Hardware Security Modules (HSM) also play a crucial role. The findings highlight the need for a multi-layered security framework combining real-time monitoring, AI-driven threat detection, and strong encryption. Collaboration between manufacturers and regulators is essential to ensure the safe and secure deployment of AV technologies. © 2025},
