	abstract = {Despite cognitive limits in surveillance (vigilance decrement, signal-noise overload), current AI prioritises automation over symbiosis, exacerbating deskilling and automation bias. Grounded in psychological modelling, we propose ALARMS (Analysis, Locutor, Anticipation, Risk Manager, Simulation)–a modular multi-agent framework mapping Endsley’s Situational Awareness (SA) levels and IDA macrocognition to specialised agents, drawing on Signal Detection Theory and vigilance research. Each agent maps to a cognitive function: situation reconstruction (Analysis–SA Levels 1–2), cognitive mirroring and explanation (Locutor–SA Level 2), weak-signal detection (Anticipation–SA Level 3), probabilistic risk support (Risk Manager), and operator training (Simulation). Human-AI collaboration uses a six-step interaction cycle and four measurable dimensions (SA gain, workload, trust calibration, task performance). ALARMS prevents deskilling via cognitive mirroring. Three scenarios (urban/transport, cybersecurity SOC, industrial SCADA) demonstrate human-centric design, with projected false-alarm reductions (up to 40%), vigilance improvements (15–20% per session), and reduced detection latency. These results suggest cognitively grounded multi-agent architectures can shift surveillance AI from opaque oracle to symbiotic partner, preserving operator agency. © 2026 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
