	abstract = {The enhanced digitalization in India has contributed significantly to the processing of people’s private data, leading to substantial data privacy and cybersecurity apprehensions. While the adoption of the Digital Personal Data Protection (DPDP) Act, 2023 can be considered an important regulatory advance, its efficiency in tackling real-life security problems still remains questionable. In this paper, one of the major issues associated with data protection in India is analyzed. Going further than the discussion of the problem under analysis and describing it, the paper will identify key drawbacks of the Indian framework regarding data protection which contribute to the lack of security results despite regulatory compliance. Specifically, the lack of appropriate enforcement, the inadequacy of organizations’ readiness to respond to the risks associated with new technologies like artificial intelligence, and the problems associated with implementing privacy policies based on informed consent will be discussed. A comparative analysis of India’s regulations and those of other countries, such as the GDPR, reveals specific aspects in which India lags behind international best practices in this area. Based on the discussion, this paper will develop recommendations for the Indian legislators and establishments in relation to the issue at hand. © Copyright 2026 Taylor & Francis–All rights reserved.},
