	abstract = {The increasing deployment of IoT-enabled Wireless Sensor Networks (WSNs) has introduced new security challenges due to their distributed nature, limited computational resources, and vulnerability to sophisticated cyber-attacks. Traditional intrusion detection systems (IDS) often suffer from high false positive rates and poor adaptability, making them unsuitable for dynamic IoT environments. This paper proposes TEAD (Trust-Enhanced Anomaly Detection), a hybrid framework that integrates trust evaluation with machine learning-based anomaly detection to enhance intrusion detection in IoT-WSNs. The TEAD framework dynamically computes trust scores for network nodes based on behavioral parameters, including Communication Link Reliability, Communication Weight Ratio, and Communication Frequency Deviation. When a node has low trust, anomaly detection is applied with the help of statistical models and LSTM and autoencoders. The model is tested against NSL-KDD and CIC-IDS-2017, and it performs better than most traditional IDS models. TEAD has an accuracy of 96%, leading to an error rate of 2% for false positives and an error rate of 3% for false negatives. Based on the results, TEAD can ensure security for resource-constrained IoT-WSNs that is flexible, scalable and respects privacy. Research efforts are planned in federated learning and Edge AI to make network security independent, prompt and capable of self-repair. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Science+Business Media, LLC, part of Springer Nature 2025.},
