	abstract = {Ransomware remains a dominant and escalating threat in the cybersecurity landscape, marked by increasingly sophisticated attack tactics and a surge in zero-day variants. Traditional detection technique struggle to address the evasive behaviors and obfuscation methods employed by modern ransomware. In response, behavior-based detection, enhanced by artificial intelligence (AI), has emerged as a promising solution. However, the development of robust AI models is hindered by the scarcity of balanced, publicly available behavioral datasets. This work addresses this gap by introducing a new public dataset of 1,042 samples, including 518 ransomware variants and 524 benign applications, spanning families from 2021–2025. Each sample is represented by 247 engineered behavioral features derived from dynamic analysis, covering API invocations, file system activity, registry modifications, and network communications. A standardized preprocessing pipeline ensures consistency, reliability, and suitability for machine learning workflows. Using this dataset, we trained and evaluated six supervised classifiers. Lightweight models such as Support Vector Machines (SVM) and Logistic Regression achieved recall rates above 97%, with SVM correctly detecting 66 of 67 ransomware samples and Logistic Regression attaining an ROC-AUC of 0.997. These results significantly exceed prior reports (\approx85–93%) and establish a new benchmark for high-recall ransomware detection. © This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License. License details: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/},
