	abstract = {There is no shortage of advice one can find on the Internet for an incoming CISO. The majority of them are driven by vendors trying to sell their products, while some are driven by the Boards. There is nothing wrong with any of this advice, apart from the fact that vendors’ advice is usually very narrowly focused, while AI or Boards’ advice is too fuzzy and generic. Vendors’ advice often comes with hidden surprises surrounding its implementation. This is not a criticism of either of the technologies per se but is just an attempt to show an example of a lack of a holistic approach when a CISO’s actions are based on the implementation of “the latest and greatest” offered by the vendors. Typically, this advice is focused on dealing with symptoms, or more accurately, on dealing with distribution mechanisms, leaving aside the root causes and their combination. And very often, this advice leads to a never-ending increase in complexity of the organisation’s IT ecosystem and supply chain, which becomes a contributing factor to the cumulative effect. The article offers a holistic, high-level view of certain aspects of cybersecurity often overlooked by technical and AI advice, which more often than not occurs due to a lack of understanding of the inherent insecurity of IT foundations and a focus on specific technical aspects, and provides guidance to CEOs and Directors. © Copyright 2026 Taylor & Francis–All rights reserved.},
	publisher = {Taylor and Francis Ltd.},
