	abstract = {Network Intrusion Detection Systems (NIDS) play a pivotal role in cybersecurity defense. However, existing Machine Learning-based approaches frequently suffer from performance degradation when confronting highly imbalanced traffic data, typically exhibiting high overall accuracy but suboptimal recall for minority attack classes. To mitigate this persistent issue, this paper proposes a lightweight and interpretable intrusion detection framework based on an optimized Random Forest algorithm. First, Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is employed to reduce feature dimensionality and computational complexity. Subsequently, a novel Multi-Threshold Evaluation (MTE) strategy is introduced to dynamically calibrate the decision boundaries for individual attack categories, effectively rectifying the bias towards the majority class without relying on computationally expensive synthetic data generation. Experimental results on the CICIDS2017 dataset demonstrate the efficacy of the proposed approach, achieving an overall Accuracy of 98.60% and a remarkable Macro F1-score of 0.9700. Compared to standard baseline models and complex Deep Learning architectures, our method significantly improves the detection robustness for rare and complex attack vectors (e.g., Web Attacks and Botnets) while maintaining low computational overhead. Furthermore, SHAP (Shapley Additive Explanations) analysis is integrated to elucidate the model's decision logic, validating that the classification relies on domain-relevant flow characteristics rather than spurious correlations. The proposed framework offers a pragmatic balance between detection precision, computational efficiency, and model transparency, making it highly suitable for real-time deployment in resource-constrained edge computing environments. © 2026 The Authors.},
