	affiliations = {Hokkaido University of Science, Sapporo, Hokkaido, 006-8585, Japan},
	abstract = {Recent studies have shown that large language models (LLMs) can be misused to generate malicious software, primarily by relying on explicit jailbreak techniques that circumvent built-in safety mechanisms. In contrast, this study investigates an underexplored misuse pathway: the generation of malware-relevant functionality through standard, policy-compliant interactions without explicit jailbreak prompts. We empirically analyze a function-oriented prompting approach and show that contemporary LLMs can produce code exhibiting malicious functionality without triggering explicit refusal responses. This demonstrates that jailbreak-free malicious emergence can arise from the incremental composition of benign-looking programming tasks. We further evaluate the detectability of executable artifacts derived from LLM-generated code using widely deployed static and dynamic analysis tools. Under the considered experimental setting and threat model, our results establish a clear boundary condition: straightforward LLM-generated executable malware remains consistently detectable, and representative source-code obfuscation techniques do not materially reduce detection rates. To assess generality, we conduct a comparative analysis of multiple LLMs, including ChatGPT-4o and Gemini 1.5 Pro. While we observe qualitative differences in code-generation behavior, jailbreak-free malicious emergence occurs across models, indicating that the identified misuse risk is not tied to a specific implementation but reflects broader characteristics of current-generation LLM-based program synthesis. Beyond empirical findings, we introduce a lightweight conceptual abstraction that formalizes jailbreak-free malicious emergence as a compositional property of function-oriented interactions. Together, these results provide a grounded characterization of current misuse risks, clarify the limits of present-day detection effectiveness, and offer a foundation for developing more robust defensive,