	abstract = {Continuous health monitoring, personalized drug delivery, nanosurgery, and related medical applications increasingly depend on networked biosensors. Enhancing biosensor performance often requires integration with high-bandwidth networks such as 5 G, demanding reliable translation from biochemical signals to electromagnetic communication. While modern sensors enable continuous monitoring of vital parameters including heart rate, blood pressure, and temperature, their reliance on wireless connectivity raises serious security concerns. This study investigates vulnerabilities within healthcare wireless infrastructures by modeling an implantable medical and patient monitoring system and conducting port scanning, distributed denial-of-service, and man-in-the-middle attack simulations that expose sensitive patient data. To address these risks, an integrated IoT–blockchain architecture supported by smart contracts is proposed for secure patient healthcare management. The framework separates core and edge networks, combining centralized efficiency with decentralized trust to improve scalability, resilience, and data integrity. Blockchain enables transparent data exchange, autonomous decision execution, and tamper resistance for wearable and implantable devices. Simulation results demonstrate effective autonomous monitoring and control under adversarial conditions. Additionally, an AI-driven threat detection model is developed to identify anomalous behavior and mitigate future attacks in real time. The proposed approach strengthens operational safety. © 2026 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.},
