	abstract = {This manuscript explores the adoption of AI-based e-commerce by Taiwanese SMEs and its impact on marketing performance expectancy. Taiwanese SMEs are vital to the economy, representing 98 % of businesses and contributing significantly to GDP. To remain competitive, these SMEs are increasingly adopting AI-driven e-commerce strategies, supported by Taiwan's advanced digital infrastructure and government initiatives like “Smart Taiwan 2025.” However, challenges such as logistics costs, technology integration, and cybersecurity remain obstacles to full adoption. The study utilizes the Technology-Organization-Environment (TOE) framework to examine the factors that influence AI adoption among Taiwanese SMEs. The research focuses on AI acceptance, online marketing capability, innovation culture, and external pressures such as competition and business partner demands. A structured questionnaire was used to gather data from 206 SMEs, and the analysis was conducted using Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM). The results indicate that AI acceptance and online marketing capabilities are significant drivers of AI adoption. Additionally, innovation culture is critical in promoting an environment conducive to adopting AI technologies. Competitive pressures and business partner demands were also identified as major factors influencing adoption, with business partner pressures being the most influential. SMEs that rely on partners for access to AI tools or data are more likely to integrate AI into their operations. © 2024},
