	abstract = {Artificial Intelligence (AI) is transforming modern energy systems by enhancing forecasting precision, optimizing grid management, and facilitating the integration of renewable energy sources. Concurrently, phishing attacks persist as a significant cybersecurity threat, underscoring the need for robust detection mechanisms. This paper examines the dual role of AI in these domains by conducting an evaluation of three widely used Machine Learning classifiers-Support Vector Machines (SVM), k-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), and Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP)-for phishing website detection. Using the Mendeley dataset comprising both phishing and legitimate websites, we benchmark the classifiers on key performance metrics, including accuracy, precision, recall, and f1-score. Our analysis reveals accuracies of 93.66% for SVM, 93.13% for KNN, and 93.88% for MLP, along with distinct differences in computational demands and practical applicability. By highlighting the strengths and limitations of each approach, this study offers valuable insights for selecting the most effective classifier for real-world phishing detection, while also contributing to the broader understanding of AI’s impact on smart energy systems. © 2025 NIPES Pub.},
