	abstract = {This paper presents a transformative cyber supply chain model, where the distribution of digital product files replaces the movement of physical goods. The product is manufactured at the time of need and close to the point of consumption using a digital file as an input. By shifting from globalized physical distribution to localized, on-demand manufacturing based on digital recipes, the model addresses vulnerabilities exposed by supply chain disruptions, such as those during the COVID-19 pandemic. Through a case study on furniture masking plugs—high-mix, low-volume products—the study demonstrates substantial economic, environmental, and operational benefits. Key outcomes include up to 99% inventory cost reduction, 17% immediate CO<sub>2</sub> emissions savings (projected to 65% by 2030), and 65% fewer backlog orders under disruptions compared to centralized systems. The framework integrates AI, blockchain, and IoT for real-time adaptability, traceability, and intellectual property protection. While highlighting the potential for scalable, eco-efficient production, the paper identifies critical challenges, including cybersecurity, quality assurance, and regulatory compliance, and outlines future research directions to bridge technical and operational gaps. This work advances the transition toward agile, sustainable supply chains in an era of deglobalization. © 2025 The Author(s)},
