	affiliations = {Foreign Research Fellow, Dalian Maritime University Law School, Liaoning, China; of Law; and Associate Director of DMU-Swansea Joint Education Programme, Dalian Maritime University Law School, Liaoning, China},
	abstract = {This review examines whether China's maritime law education system is prepared to meet the demands of a rapidly evolving maritime industry shaped by sustainability targets, digital transformation, and artificial intelligence integration. Among others, these shifts introduce complex legal and regulatory challenges such as new frameworks for carbon accountability, data governance, and autonomous vessel operations. To assess educational alignment where these changes, the review follows a two-step approach: first, it draws on academic and policy literature to identify emerging technologies and the associated legal competencies needed to navigate their regulatory impact; second, it then evaluates the extent to which China's maritime law curricula address these professional requirements. As this review aims to highlight, much scholarly and technical attention has been given to the operational and environmental dimensions of maritime innovation, there remains a professional skills gap – a disconnect between the competences now demanded by industry and those currently taught in legal education. Through comparative curriculum analysis, the paper highlights opportunities for Chinese institutions to proactively leapfrog integrate interdisciplinary legal training in areas such as maritime environmental governance, cybersecurity, and AI liability. The student concludes with a phased proposal for curriculum reform aimed at strengthening China's legal capacity in the global maritime domain. © 2025 The Authors},
