	abstract = {Cache side-channel attacks (CSCAs) leverage shared hardware resources to leak sensitive data, bypassing traditional security measures. While existing approaches have shown promise in detecting these attacks, they often suffer from overfitting and are evaluated on a limited range of attacks. Moreover, classification of attacks remains understudied, despite its critical role in enabling targeted countermeasures and improving the response to new variants. This study provides a detailed comparative and theoretical analysis of ensemble learning models–LightGBM, XGBoost, Gradient Boosting, CatBoost and Random Forest–for CSCA classification. The novelty of this study is in evaluating the effectiveness of ensemble models in multiclass classification of a diverse range of attacks. XGBoost outperforms other models, achieving 99.8% accuracy and 99% recall while effectively handling evasive attack patterns. Through robust hyperparameter tuning and loss function optimization, this study shows how XGBoost’s feature interaction handling and regularization make it ideal for this application. The evaluation spans a broad range of CSCAs, expanding beyond standard Prime+Probe and Flush+Reload attacks, ensuring practical applicability. SHAP-based explainability is used to analyze feature importance across attacks. The results establish ensemble learning as a powerful approach for hardware-based threat detection and set a benchmark for future ML-driven CSCA defense strategies. © 2025 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
