	abstract = {This paper analyses the complex interplay between cyber-victimization, anger and frustration, whereby mediatory roles of artificial intelligence in cybersecurity and e-governance and the moderating role of cyberbullying coping mechanisms. The research was conducted in the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia with a focus on growing challenges of cyber threats to professionals and reviews technological and institutional solutions to mitigate these impacts. A quantitative research design was utilized, with a sample size of 244 employees working in different departments of Saudi Arabia. Data were gathered using validated scales and were analyzed using Structural Equation Modeling (SEM) through the use of Stata software. Reliability and validity were ensured by Cronbach's alpha, composite reliability, and average variance extracted, ensuring robust findings. The results show that cyber-victimization has a significant impact on the anger and frustration of employees. AI in cybersecurity and e-governance was found to effectively mediate this relationship, reducing negative emotional responses. Moreover, coping mechanisms moderated these effects, which underlines the importance of coping mechanisms in digital resilience. The study highlights the critical role of integrating technological and institutional frameworks to address psychological consequences of cyber threats. This research provides new insights into the management of cyber-victimization's impact in organizational settings, focusing on the synergy between advanced technologies and e-governance. It provides practical strategies for building resilience in digitally exposed workplaces. Copyright © 2024 International Journal of Cyber Criminology},
