	abstract = {Cryptojacking, the illicit use of computational power for cryptocurrency mining, poses a serious threat in web and IoT environments, leading to degraded performance, increased energy consumption, and potential equipment damage. This paper introduces CryptojackGuard, a novel multimodal framework that integrates large language models (LLMs) for semantic script analysis, graph neural networks (GNNs) for behavioral anomaly detection, Time-Aware Transformers for temporal attack phase identification, and SHAP-based explainable AI (XAI) for interpretability. Using public datasets (5,000 samples from arXiv, GitHub, and Kaggle), the system achieves 99.8% accuracy, a 0.998 F1-score, 0.03 MAE in prediction, and 98% SHAP coverage, with low Shannon entropy values indicating high confidence in its predictions. The system employs attention-based fusion to integrate heterogeneous modalities—code snippets, network flows, and time-series metrics—enabling zero-shot detection of obfuscated variants and proactive phase prediction (e.g., injection, throttling). Ablation experiments confirm component synergy, with the fusion layer contributing significantly to performance gains. Comparative evaluation shows superior performance over baselines such as Crypto-Aegis (F1-score: 0.96), eBPF-based detection (99.75% accuracy), and CryptojackingTrap (near-zero false positives but reliant on blockchain). CryptojackGuard effectively addresses gaps in semantic analysis, multimodal fusion, zero-shot capability, interpretability, scalability, and evasion robustness. The key contributions of this work are: 1) integration of LLM, GNN, and Transformer models for comprehensive detection; 2) zero-shot adaptability to unseen variants; 3) temporal prediction for proactive mitigation; 4) attention-based alignment for efficient information exchange; and 5) SHAP-integrated XAI for enhanced explainability. CryptojackGuard thus provides an interpretable and scalable solution for real-time cryptojacking detection in resource-constrained environments. © 2013 IEEE.},
