	affiliations = {National Yunlin University of Science and Technology, Yunlin, Douliu, 64002, Taiwan},
	abstract = {In today’s increasingly severe cybersecurity threat landscape, threat intelligence from a single source can no longer fully reflect the actual risks faced by a device. This study proposes a device trust evaluation framework that integrates both structured and unstructured threat intelligence. Through feature matching and Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP), the trust score of a device is calculated to dynamically reflect its level of trustworthiness. Structured threat intelligence is obtained from the CISA KEV database, while unstructured intelligence is extracted using the large language model LLAMA 3.1 from public news websites such as SecurityWeek and Krebs on Security. Device-side features are collected via Osquery and OpenVAS scans, including operating system versions, open ports, CVE vulnerabilities, installed software, and executables. All features are converted into structured formats for matching and are weighted using AHP to compute the trust score. In addition, this study designs three experimental scenarios, each adopting different threat intelligence integration strategies to observe their impact on trust scores. Experimental results show that scenarios combining both structured and unstructured intelligence with the most complete information can effectively reveal hidden risks of devices and provide more accurate trust judgment. © 2013 IEEE.},
